am"},{"id":"C","text":"be"},{"id":"D","text":"is"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Use how many before plural countable nouns and how much before uncountable nouns. Use plural agreement with the former and singular agreement with the latter. Focus on **noun agreement** in this question.","explanation":"The uncountable noun soap takes is in this sentence.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"noun-agreement","contextKey":"quantity-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-countable-uncountable"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"bd144e6d6d22f2d7b34f15cfa3f4af6ff4eb23019856805c8c78b2811f5ff4a5","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **noun agreement**. Use how many before plural countable nouns and how much before uncountable nouns. Use plural agreement with the former and singular agreement with the latter.","The uncountable noun soap takes is in this sentence.","Choose **is**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**are** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. The uncountable noun soap takes is in this sentence.","B":"**am** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. The uncountable noun soap takes is in this sentence.","C":"Base **be** does not supply the finite agreement required here. The uncountable noun soap takes is in this sentence.","D":"The uncountable noun soap takes is in this sentence."}}},{"id":"18230cb4-d2af-56c7-a1ee-77e24ac8c972","topicSlug":"countable-uncountable","level":"A1","prompt":"Use “some” to refer to soap in the washing container, in the sense of measuring liquid soap. Choose the complete noun phrase.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"some soap"},{"id":"B","text":"some of soap"},{"id":"C","text":"some a soap"},{"id":"D","text":"a some soap"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Some works with both plural countable and uncountable nouns. For an uncountable noun, add a unit phrase when counting individual quantities. Focus on **quantity phrase** in this question.","explanation":"Some goes directly before soap. Some of needs an identified set, for example “some of the soap”.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"quantity-phrase","contextKey":"quantity-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-countable-uncountable"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"ca9033fe13361e977518723e72a90c4a1469fd2b1d8265d02847462c90850a96","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **quantity phrase**. Some works with both plural countable and uncountable nouns. For an uncountable noun, add a unit phrase when counting individual quantities.","Some goes directly before soap. Some of needs an identified set, for example “some of the soap”.","Choose **some soap**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Some goes directly before soap. Some of needs an identified set, for example “some of the soap”.","B":"Some of needs a determined set, such as some of the ...; the required determiner after of is missing.","C":"This puts two incompatible determiners together. Use **some directly before the noun** in the requested phrase. Some goes directly before soap. Some of needs an identified set, for example “some of the soap”.","D":"This puts two incompatible determiners together. Use **some directly before the noun** in the requested phrase. Some goes directly before soap. Some of needs an identified set, for example “some of the soap”."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Countability is a property of a noun’s meaning in context, not a judgement about whether real objects can physically be counted. Furniture refers to a collection as an uncountable concept; chairs names separate countable units. The choice affects articles, quantity words and verb agreement."],"rules":[{"title":"Count the things or measure an amount","explanation":"Countable nouns name separate units and can be plural. Uncountable nouns name amounts or collections in the senses used here. Other senses may differ.","examples":["three baskets","some equipment","The **information is** useful."],"pattern":"**a chair / two chairs** · **some furniture**","paragraphs":["A singular count noun usually needs a determiner such as a, this or my. A plural count noun can be used generally without one. An uncountable noun does not normally take a/an in its uncountable sense. Add a unit phrase when needed: a bottle of water."]},{"title":"Asking about quantity","explanation":"Use how many before plural countable nouns and how much before uncountable nouns. Use plural agreement with the former and singular agreement with the latter.","examples":["How many baskets are ready?","How much equipment is available?","There are **three pieces of equipment**."],"pattern":"**How many chairs?** / **How much furniture?**","paragraphs":["Use many with plural count nouns and much with uncountable nouns. A lot of works with both and is often more natural in affirmative everyday statements. Plural count nouns take plural agreement; uncountable subjects normally take singular agreement."]},{"title":"Some and individual units","explanation":"Some works with both plural countable and uncountable nouns. For an uncountable noun, add a unit phrase when counting individual quantities.","examples":["some luggage","two pieces of luggage","How **many** bags? How **much** luggage?"],"pattern":"**a piece of advice** / **some advice**","paragraphs":["Some works with plural counts and uncountable amounts. To count an uncountable quantity, identify an appropriate unit: two pieces of information, three slices of bread. The unit noun becomes plural while the uncountable noun remains unchanged."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"A noun may change countability with its sense. Chicken can mean an animal or meat; coffee can mean a substance or a serving; paper can mean a material or a newspaper/article. The exercises state the intended sense so that a valid alternate meaning is not unfairly marked wrong.","examples":["The **information is** useful.","There are **three pieces of equipment**.","How **many** bags? How **much** luggage?"]}],"recap":["**a chair / two chairs** · **some furniture**","**How many chairs?** / **How much furniture?**","**a piece of advice** / **some advice**"]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["present-continuous"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
